{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V3AJBra9LTLdfwndFDmmxC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## A01 G1 Distinct agent identity | lead=infrastructure\n2\tREQ: Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n3\tNA: never\n4\tNOTCOV: Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02; Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies->A04; A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed->B02; Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured->B04\n5\t - A01.1 The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n6\t   V: 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n7\t - A01.2 Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n8\t   V: 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n9\t - A01.3 When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n10\t   V: 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n11\t - A01.4 Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n12\t   V: 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n13\t## A02 G1 All six identity fields | lead=agent_team\n14\tREQ: Check that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n15\tNA: never\n16\tNOTCOV: Identity labels taken from what the model supplies->A04; Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs->A03; Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10; Sub-agent record missing its parent link or parent prompt->A06\n17\t - A02.1 The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n18\t   V: 1) Find the audit record schema or the function that writes audit entries. 2) Check for each of the six: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, trace context (`traceparent` or trace and span IDs). 3) Answer no if any one is missing from the schema or never set.\n19\t - A02.2 All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n20\t   V: 1) List every path that writes an audit record, or should: success, policy deny, `except`/`catch` blocks, retries, queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks, other endpoints such as approvals, and refusals before the run starts (quota, rate limit, auth) that return an error without logging. 2) For each, check all six fields are set, ideally by one shared wrapper. 3) Answer no if any deny, error, async, or background path writes records without them, or writes no record at all.\n21\t - A02.3 Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n22\t   V: 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check it stores trace and span IDs (or `traceparent`) as fields in the record itself. 3) Answer no if trace context exists only in OpenTelemetry spans, or the record's trace field is never filled.\n23\t - A02.4 Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n24\t   V: 1) Find where tenant and owner values for audit records are read. 2) Trace each back to its source: token claims, verified session, IAM tags, or SPIFFE path, versus OTel baggage, `tracestate`, headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, request body, or tool arguments. 3) Answer no if any path takes them from an unprotected source.\n25\t## A03 G1 Content-derived type identity | lead=agent_team\n26\tREQ: Compute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n27\tNA: never\n28\tNOTCOV: Changed running config still reporting the old approved ID->C03; Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; Release manifest missing training job, dataset, or model provider records->C01; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n29\t - A03.1 The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n30\t   V: 1) Find the code or CI step that computes `agent.type.id` (search `sha256`, `hashlib`, `createHash`, `type_id`). 2) List its inputs. 3) Check each is there: container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, configuration. 4) Answer no if the ID is a label, version string, or git tag, or any input is missing.\n31\t - A03.2 The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n32\t   V: 1) Open the function that lists files to hash. 2) Write down every filter it applies: hidden files, names starting with '_' or '__', extensions, ignore lists, glob patterns. 3) For each filter, check whether a file that defines tools, prompts, or config would be skipped (for example `__init__.py`, `index.ts`, `.env`, `config/*.local.*`). 4) Answer no if any such file is skipped.\n33\t - A03.3 The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n34\t   V: 1) Find the model ID that goes into the manifest or hash. 2) Follow env vars and config to the final value, including defaults such as `getenv('MODEL', ...)`. 3) Answer no if it is an alias (`-latest`, no date or version), falls back to one when unset, or is the app release number. Also no if adapters or checkpoints are used but left out.\n35\t - A03.4 The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID.\n36\t   V: 1) Open the serialization step before hashing. 2) Check for a fixed order: sorted keys (`sort_keys=True`), sorted file lists, fixed encoding and line endings. 3) Check file lists are not taken straight from directory listing or map iteration order. 4) Answer no if any unsorted dict, map, or filesystem order reaches the hash.\n37\t - A03.5 Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n38\t   V: 1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it.\n39\t## A04 G1 Trustworthy attribution | lead=infrastructure\n40\tREQ: Assign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n41\tNA: never\n42\tNOTCOV: Agent sharing the launching user's identity->A01; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06; Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority->E08; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n43\t - A04.1 Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n44\t   V: 1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context.\n45\t - A04.2 Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n46\t   V: 1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues.\n47\t - A04.3 The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n48\t   V: 1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled.\n49\t - A04.4 When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n50\t   V: 1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data.\n51\t - A04.5 Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n52\t   V: 1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions.\n53\t## A05 G1 Operational lookup | lead=operations\n54\tREQ: Start from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n55\tNA: never\n56\tNOTCOV: Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time->R11; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n57\t - A05.1 Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n58\t   V: 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n59\t - A05.2 An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n60\t   V: 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n61\t - A05.3 A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n62\t   V: 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n63\t## A06 G3 Parent and prompt provenance | lead=agent_team\n64\tREQ: For every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n65\tNA: The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n66\tNOTCOV: Sub-agent given powers beyond its task->E08; Children still running after the parent is stopped->E09; No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start->B07\n67\t - A06.1 Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n68\t   V: 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n69\t - A06.2 Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n70\t   V: 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n71\t - A06.3 The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n72\t   V: 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n73\t - A06.4 Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n74\t   V: 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n75\t## A07 G3 Every model in the loop | lead=agent_team\n76\tREQ: List all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n77\tNA: The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n78\tNOTCOV: Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed->C05; Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior->R10; Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check->C05\n79\t - A07.1 A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n80\t   V: 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n81\t - A07.2 Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n82\t   V: 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n83\t - A07.3 Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n84\t   V: 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n85\t - A07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n86\t   V: 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n87\t## R01 G2 Validate every input boundary, including API responses | lead=agent_team\n88\tREQ: List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n89\tNA: never\n90\tNOTCOV: Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked->R02; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04; Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check->E07; Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs->R03; Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus->R04\n91\t - R01.1 Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n92\t   V: 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n93\t - R01.2 Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n94\t   V: 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n95\t - R01.3 Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n96\t   V: 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n97\t - R01.4 Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n98\t   V: 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n99\t - R01.5 Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n100\t   V: 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n101\t## R02 G2 Separate data from instructions and test injection containment | lead=security\n102\tREQ: Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n103\tNA: never\n104\tNOTCOV: Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses->R01; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04; One run planting instructions in another run's memory->R05; Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing->R03\n105\t - R02.1 Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n106\t   V: 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n107\t - R02.2 Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n108\t   V: 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n109\t - R02.3 Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n110\t   V: 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n111\t - R02.4 Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n112\t   V: 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n113\t - R02.5 Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n114\t   V: 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n115\t## R03 G2 Prevent output and log leakage | lead=agent_team\n116\tREQ: Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n117\tNA: never\n118\tNOTCOV: A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination->B09; Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images->B03; Incoming hostile payloads not validated->R01; Retrieval returning another tenant's documents->R04\n119\t - R03.1 Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n120\t   V: 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n121\t - R03.2 The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n122\t   V: 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n123\t - R03.3 Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n124\t   V: 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n125\t - R03.4 Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n126\t   V: 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n127\t## R04 G2 Retrieval-corpus integrity | lead=agent_team\n128\tREQ: The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n129\tNA: Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n130\tNOTCOV: Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants->R05; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02; Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions->R01; Embedding or reranker model swapped without review->C05\n131\t - R04.1 Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n132\t   V: 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n133\t - R04.2 Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n134\t   V: 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n135\t - R04.3 Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n136\t   V: 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n137\t - R04.4 Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n138\t   V: 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n139\t## R05 G3 Memory isolation and write validation | lead=agent_team\n140\tREQ: Separate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n141\tNA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n142\tNOTCOV: No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it->R06; Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant->R04; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02; Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked->C07\n143\t - R05.1 Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n144\t   V: 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n145\t - R05.2 Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n146\t   V: 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n147\t - R05.3 The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n148\t   V: 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n149\t - R05.4 Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n150\t   V: 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n151\t## R06 G3 Memory provenance and cleanup | lead=agent_team\n152\tREQ: For any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n153\tNA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n154\tNOTCOV: Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task->R05; Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired->R04; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n155\t - R06.1 Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n156\t   V: 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n157\t - R06.2 Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n158\t   V: 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n159\t - R06.3 Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n160\t   V: 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n161\t - R06.4 Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n162\t   V: 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n163\t## R07 G2 Decision-time context size | lead=agent_team\n164\tREQ: For each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n165\tNA: never\n166\tNOTCOV: Behavior not compared across context-size ranges->R09; Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n167\t - R07.1 Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n168\t   V: 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n169\t - R07.2 Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n170\t   V: 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n171\t - R07.3 The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n172\t   V: 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n173\t - R07.4 The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n174\t   V: 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n175\t## R08 G3 Security monitoring | lead=security\n176\tREQ: Keep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n177\tNA: never\n178\tNOTCOV: Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09; No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior->R10; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12; Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07\n179\t - R08.1 Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n180\t   V: 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n181\t - R08.2 Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n182\t   V: 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n183\t - R08.3 Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n184\t   V: 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n185\t## R09 G3 Sequence and context-aware detection | lead=security\n186\tREQ: Test suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\n187\tNA: never\n188\tNOTCOV: Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected->R08; No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent->R10; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07\n189\t - R09.1 Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n190\t   V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n191\t - R09.2 Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n192\t   V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n193\t - R09.3 Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n194\t   V: 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n195\t - R09.4 If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n196\t   V: 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n197\t## R10 G3 Misaligned-behavior review | lead=security\n198\tREQ: If deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\n199\tNA: Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\n200\tNOTCOV: Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate->B05; A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure->A07; Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09\n201\t## R11 G1 Tested kill switch | lead=operations\n202\tREQ: Name who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\n203\tNA: never\n204\tNOTCOV: Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers->E09; In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop->R12; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04\n205\t - R11.1 A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n206\t   V: 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n207\t - R11.2 The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n208\t   V: 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n209\t - R11.3 Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n210\t   V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n211\t - R11.4 The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n212\t   V: 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n213\t - R11.5 Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n214\t   V: 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n215\t## R12 G1 Safe state after stopping | lead=agent_team\n216\tREQ: Decide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\n217\tNA: never\n218\tNOTCOV: Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time->R11; Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints->R14; Rollback restoring revoked permissions->C07\n219\t - R12.1 For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n220\t   V: 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n221\t - R12.2 On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n222\t   V: 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n223\t - R12.3 Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n224\t   V: 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n225\t - R12.4 A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n226\t   V: 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n227\t - R12.5 Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n228\t   V: 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n229\t## R13 G1 Full execution audit | lead=agent_team\n230\tREQ: Record each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\n231\tNA: never\n232\tNOTCOV: Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02; Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10; Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n233\t - R13.1 Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n234\t   V: 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n235\t - R13.2 Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n236\t   V: 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n237\t - R13.3 Approved actions record their approval ID.\n238\t   V: 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n239\t - R13.4 Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n240\t   V: 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n241\t - R13.5 Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n242\t   V: 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n243\t## R14 G2 Recovery integrity | lead=agent_team\n244\tREQ: If logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\n245\tNA: Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\n246\tNOTCOV: What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop->R12; Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested->C07; Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history->E10\n247\t - R14.1 Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n248\t   V: 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n249\t - R14.2 Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n250\t   V: 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n251\t - R14.3 Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n252\t   V: 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n253\t - R14.4 Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n254\t   V: 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n255\t - R14.5 When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n256\t   V: 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.\n257\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9a932460-770f-4311-8553-70e3bee77013","uuid":"84ba5381-713f-49cd-b53d-5b84e374ee50","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:00:39.842Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/RA.txt","content":"## A01 G1 Distinct agent identity | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02; Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies->A04; A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed->B02; Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured->B04\n - A01.1 The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n   V: 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n - A01.2 Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n   V: 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n - A01.3 When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n   V: 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n - A01.4 Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n   V: 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n## A02 G1 All six identity fields | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Check that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Identity labels taken from what the model supplies->A04; Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs->A03; Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10; Sub-agent record missing its parent link or parent prompt->A06\n - A02.1 The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n   V: 1) Find the audit record schema or the function that writes audit entries. 2) Check for each of the six: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, trace context (`traceparent` or trace and span IDs). 3) Answer no if any one is missing from the schema or never set.\n - A02.2 All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n   V: 1) List every path that writes an audit record, or should: success, policy deny, `except`/`catch` blocks, retries, queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks, other endpoints such as approvals, and refusals before the run starts (quota, rate limit, auth) that return an error without logging. 2) For each, check all six fields are set, ideally by one shared wrapper. 3) Answer no if any deny, error, async, or background path writes records without them, or writes no record at all.\n - A02.3 Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n   V: 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check it stores trace and span IDs (or `traceparent`) as fields in the record itself. 3) Answer no if trace context exists only in OpenTelemetry spans, or the record's trace field is never filled.\n - A02.4 Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n   V: 1) Find where tenant and owner values for audit records are read. 2) Trace each back to its source: token claims, verified session, IAM tags, or SPIFFE path, versus OTel baggage, `tracestate`, headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, request body, or tool arguments. 3) Answer no if any path takes them from an unprotected source.\n## A03 G1 Content-derived type identity | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Compute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Changed running config still reporting the old approved ID->C03; Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; Release manifest missing training job, dataset, or model provider records->C01; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n - A03.1 The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n   V: 1) Find the code or CI step that computes `agent.type.id` (search `sha256`, `hashlib`, `createHash`, `type_id`). 2) List its inputs. 3) Check each is there: container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, configuration. 4) Answer no if the ID is a label, version string, or git tag, or any input is missing.\n - A03.2 The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n   V: 1) Open the function that lists files to hash. 2) Write down every filter it applies: hidden files, names starting with '_' or '__', extensions, ignore lists, glob patterns. 3) For each filter, check whether a file that defines tools, prompts, or config would be skipped (for example `__init__.py`, `index.ts`, `.env`, `config/*.local.*`). 4) Answer no if any such file is skipped.\n - A03.3 The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n   V: 1) Find the model ID that goes into the manifest or hash. 2) Follow env vars and config to the final value, including defaults such as `getenv('MODEL', ...)`. 3) Answer no if it is an alias (`-latest`, no date or version), falls back to one when unset, or is the app release number. Also no if adapters or checkpoints are used but left out.\n - A03.4 The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID.\n   V: 1) Open the serialization step before hashing. 2) Check for a fixed order: sorted keys (`sort_keys=True`), sorted file lists, fixed encoding and line endings. 3) Check file lists are not taken straight from directory listing or map iteration order. 4) Answer no if any unsorted dict, map, or filesystem order reaches the hash.\n - A03.5 Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n   V: 1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it.\n## A04 G1 Trustworthy attribution | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Assign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Agent sharing the launching user's identity->A01; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06; Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority->E08; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n - A04.1 Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n   V: 1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context.\n - A04.2 Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n   V: 1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues.\n - A04.3 The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n   V: 1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled.\n - A04.4 When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n   V: 1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data.\n - A04.5 Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n   V: 1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions.\n## A05 G1 Operational lookup | lead=operations\nREQ: Start from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time->R11; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n - A05.1 Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n   V: 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n - A05.2 An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n   V: 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n - A05.3 A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n   V: 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n## A06 G3 Parent and prompt provenance | lead=agent_team\nREQ: For every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\nNA: The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\nNOTCOV: Sub-agent given powers beyond its task->E08; Children still running after the parent is stopped->E09; No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start->B07\n - A06.1 Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n   V: 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n - A06.2 Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n   V: 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n - A06.3 The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n   V: 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n - A06.4 Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n   V: 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n## A07 G3 Every model in the loop | lead=agent_team\nREQ: List all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\nNA: The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\nNOTCOV: Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed->C05; Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior->R10; Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check->C05\n - A07.1 A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n   V: 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n - A07.2 Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n   V: 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n - A07.3 Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n   V: 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n - A07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n   V: 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n## R01 G2 Validate every input boundary, including API responses | lead=agent_team\nREQ: List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked->R02; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04; Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check->E07; Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs->R03; Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus->R04\n - R01.1 Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n   V: 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n - R01.2 Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n   V: 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n - R01.3 Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n   V: 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n - R01.4 Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n   V: 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n - R01.5 Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n   V: 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n## R02 G2 Separate data from instructions and test injection containment | lead=security\nREQ: Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses->R01; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04; One run planting instructions in another run's memory->R05; Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing->R03\n - R02.1 Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n   V: 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n - R02.2 Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n   V: 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n - R02.3 Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n   V: 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n - R02.4 Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n   V: 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n - R02.5 Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n   V: 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n## R03 G2 Prevent output and log leakage | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination->B09; Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images->B03; Incoming hostile payloads not validated->R01; Retrieval returning another tenant's documents->R04\n - R03.1 Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n   V: 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n - R03.2 The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n   V: 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n - R03.3 Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n   V: 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n - R03.4 Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n   V: 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n## R04 G2 Retrieval-corpus integrity | lead=agent_team\nREQ: The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\nNA: Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\nNOTCOV: Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants->R05; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02; Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions->R01; Embedding or reranker model swapped without review->C05\n - R04.1 Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n   V: 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n - R04.2 Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n   V: 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n - R04.3 Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n   V: 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n - R04.4 Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n   V: 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n## R05 G3 Memory isolation and write validation | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Separate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\nNA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nNOTCOV: No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it->R06; Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant->R04; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02; Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked->C07\n - R05.1 Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n   V: 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n - R05.2 Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n   V: 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n - R05.3 The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n   V: 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n - R05.4 Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n   V: 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n## R06 G3 Memory provenance and cleanup | lead=agent_team\nREQ: For any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\nNA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nNOTCOV: Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task->R05; Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired->R04; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n - R06.1 Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n   V: 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n - R06.2 Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n   V: 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n - R06.3 Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n   V: 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n - R06.4 Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n   V: 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n## R07 G2 Decision-time context size | lead=agent_team\nREQ: For each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Behavior not compared across context-size ranges->R09; Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n - R07.1 Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n   V: 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n - R07.2 Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n   V: 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n - R07.3 The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n   V: 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n - R07.4 The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n   V: 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n## R08 G3 Security monitoring | lead=security\nREQ: Keep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09; No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior->R10; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12; Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07\n - R08.1 Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n   V: 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n - R08.2 Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n   V: 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n - R08.3 Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n   V: 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n## R09 G3 Sequence and context-aware detection | lead=security\nREQ: Test suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected->R08; No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent->R10; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07\n - R09.1 Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n   V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n - R09.2 Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n   V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n - R09.3 Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n   V: 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n - R09.4 If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n   V: 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n## R10 G3 Misaligned-behavior review | lead=security\nREQ: If deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\nNA: Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\nNOTCOV: Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate->B05; A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure->A07; Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09\n## R11 G1 Tested kill switch | lead=operations\nREQ: Name who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers->E09; In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop->R12; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04\n - R11.1 A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n   V: 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n - R11.2 The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n   V: 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n - R11.3 Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n   V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n - R11.4 The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n   V: 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n - R11.5 Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n   V: 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n## R12 G1 Safe state after stopping | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Decide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time->R11; Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints->R14; Rollback restoring revoked permissions->C07\n - R12.1 For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n   V: 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n - R12.2 On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n   V: 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n - R12.3 Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n   V: 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n - R12.4 A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n   V: 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n - R12.5 Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n   V: 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n## R13 G1 Full execution audit | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Record each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02; Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10; Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n - R13.1 Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n   V: 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n - R13.2 Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n   V: 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n - R13.3 Approved actions record their approval ID.\n   V: 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n - R13.4 Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n   V: 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n - R13.5 Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n   V: 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n## R14 G2 Recovery integrity | lead=agent_team\nREQ: If logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\nNA: Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\nNOTCOV: What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop->R12; Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested->C07; Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history->E10\n - R14.1 Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n   V: 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n - R14.2 Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n   V: 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n - R14.3 Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n   V: 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n - R14.4 Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n   V: 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n - R14.5 When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n   V: 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.\n","numLines":257,"startLine":1,"totalLines":257}}}
