{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GU4BkxSrrYtjxvrQnn2786","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## C01 G2 Complete release manifest | lead=agent_team\n2\tREQ: Write a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.\n3\tNA: never\n4\tNOTCOV: Running setup not checked against the approved manifest->C03; Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author->C02; Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed->C04; Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs->A03\n5\t - C01.1 A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions.\n6\t   V: 1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.\n7\t - C01.2 The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version.\n8\t   V: 1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.\n9\t - C01.3 Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used.\n10\t   V: 1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned.\n11\t - C01.4 Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number.\n12\t   V: 1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available.\n13\t - C01.5 For training the team runs itself, the manifest records the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.\n14\t   V: 1) Search for training or fine-tuning code (training jobs, `Trainer`, fine-tune API calls, pipeline configs). 2) If found, check the manifest records job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. 3) Answer no if training runs here and any field is missing. Answer unknown if training may run in another repo.\n15\t## C02 G2 Review security-relevant changes | lead=governance\n16\tREQ: Require diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies. Show who is allowed to change production config. Record emergency changes with an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n17\tNA: never\n18\tNOTCOV: Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed->C04; Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases->C06; No tested rollback to an approved setup->C07\n19\t - C02.1 The manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files live in version control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author.\n20\t   V: 1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service.\n21\t - C02.2 Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration.\n22\t   V: 1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment.\n23\t - C02.3 An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n24\t   V: 1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo.\n25\t## C03 G1 Match release identity to running state | lead=infrastructure\n26\tREQ: Freeze the artifacts that define identity for each release. Check the running setup against the approved manifest, and emit the matching agent-type-id. Include the digest and signature of weights and adapters you host yourself. Test that a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup can't keep showing the old approved identity without being caught.\n27\tNA: never\n28\tNOTCOV: Manifest missing helper models, budgets, or other components->C01; Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs->A03; Container image not signature-checked at admission->B10\n29\t - C03.1 Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags.\n30\t   V: 1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one.\n31\t - C03.2 A process outside the agent observes the running artifacts (image digest, prompt, model, tool setup) and compares them with the approved manifest, rather than trusting what the agent reports.\n32\t   V: 1) Find a process outside the agent (admission controller, CI check, sidecar, scheduled job) that reads running artifacts. 2) Check it observes image digest, prompt, model, and tool setup itself and compares each with the approved manifest. 3) Answer no if it trusts values the agent reports, or compares a value with itself. Answer unknown if it would run in a platform outside this repo.\n33\t - C03.3 The emitted agent-type-id is computed from the observed running artifacts, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup produces a different ID instead of the approved one.\n34\t   V: 1) Find where the emitted `agent.type.id` comes from. 2) Check it is computed from the observed running artifacts at start or deploy. 3) Answer no if it is a constant, an env var or config value set at build, or copied from the approved manifest, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup would still emit the approved ID.\n35\t - C03.4 Self-hosted weights and adapters are checked by digest and signature at load or deploy.\n36\t   V: 1) Find where self-hosted weights and adapters are loaded or deployed (`from_pretrained`, `torch.load`, `safetensors`, download steps). 2) Check a digest compare and a signature check run before use. 3) Answer no if either is missing, wrapped in `try/except` that continues, or a second load path skips it. If only hosted models are used, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n37\t - C03.5 A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it.\n38\t   V: 1) Find the code that handles a manifest or digest mismatch. 2) Check it blocks or quarantines: non-zero exit, admission deny, refusal to start, scale to zero. 3) Answer no if the mismatch branch only logs, warns, or emits a metric and continues, or an error in the check lets deployment proceed.\n39\t## C04 G2 Detect drift per agent | lead=operations\n40\tREQ: Compare running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against their approved baseline. Simulate a prompt edit, an added tool, and a looser egress rule. Check that each one raises an alert and gets the documented response — block, quarantine, or rollback — within a set time.\n41\tNA: never\n42\tNOTCOV: Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; A tool server changing its description or schema after approval->E03; Who may change production config, and emergency change records->C02; Manifest missing components that define the release->C01\n43\t - C04.1 A drift check compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against the approved baseline, with each of these covered.\n44\t   V: 1) Find the drift check job or tool. 2) List what it compares: images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, self-hosted weights. 3) Check each is compared with the approved baseline, not with the live state itself. 4) Answer no if any category is missing or excluded. Answer unknown if drift detection runs in an outside platform.\n45\t - C04.2 The drift check runs per agent deployment, on a schedule or on change events.\n46\t   V: 1) Find what triggers the drift check: cron or CI schedule, admission webhook, or change events. 2) Check it runs for each agent deployment, not once for the fleet or only by hand. 3) Answer no if it is manual-only or covers only some deployments. Answer unknown if scheduling is set outside this repo.\n47\t - C04.3 Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit.\n48\t   V: 1) Open the drift check's result handling. 2) Check drift raises an alert and triggers a response set in config: block, quarantine, or rollback. 3) Check a time limit is set. 4) Answer no if drift only logs, the response or time limit is missing, or a check error is treated as no drift.\n49\t## C05 G2 Control dependency, model, and training changes | lead=agent_team\n50\tREQ: Pin dependency versions. Where supported, also pin each model's base version, checkpoint or weights, and fine-tune or adapter version. Before promotion, link any changed training run, dataset, or training code or config to the model it produced and to its test results. Record the model version actually served, when the runtime or provider shares it, separately from the name you requested. Before loading weights, adapters, or datasets you host, check their signature against an approved signer (for example, with OpenSSF Model Signing). Also check their file format. Pickle-based formats can run code when loaded, so they need an approved exception and a scan. Test swapping a model, checkpoint, or adapter, and check that it triggers review and re-testing. Some remote services or names can change behind a fixed name. For those, record missing training and version details clearly. Watch available version metadata and provider notices, and decide when to re-test or stop using the model. A local config hash alone can't prove remote behavior is unchanged. A pinned snapshot fixes the weights, not the provider's serving systems, which can change under the same ID. Keep scheduled re-testing.\n51\tNA: never\n52\tNOTCOV: Tool or MCP server approved without reviewing publisher, permissions, or vulnerabilities->E02; Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked->B10; Rollback setup naming a hosted model that is retired->C07\n53\t - C05.1 Dependency versions are pinned with exact versions or hashes in lockfiles, with no ranges or floating tags.\n54\t   V: 1) Open every dependency file and lockfile (`requirements*.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`) plus Dockerfile `FROM` and install lines. 2) Look for ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`, `*`), missing lockfiles, and floating tags. 3) Answer no if any dependency is unpinned, including install commands in Dockerfiles or CI scripts that bypass the lockfile.\n55\t - C05.2 Model IDs in config are pinned snapshots (plus checkpoint, weights, and adapter versions where supported), not aliases such as 'latest'.\n56\t   V: 1) Find every model ID in config, env files, and code defaults. 2) Follow env vars to their fallback value. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one when unset, or leaves out the checkpoint, weights, or adapter version where one is used and the provider supports pinning it.\n57\t - C05.3 Self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets have their signatures checked against an approved signer before loading, and loading stops if the check fails.\n58\t   V: 1) Find load paths for self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets. 2) Check a signature check against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing, `cosign verify` with a pinned identity) runs before loading. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, any signer is accepted, or a failure is caught and loading continues. If nothing is self-hosted, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n59\t - C05.4 Pickle-based model formats are refused at load unless an approved exception and scan are recorded.\n60\t   V: 1) Search for pickle-capable loads: `pickle.load`, `torch.load`, `joblib.load`, `.pkl`, `.pt`, `.bin` files. 2) Check the loader refuses these formats unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. 3) Answer no if any pickle-based load runs without that check.\n61\t - C05.5 The model version the provider or runtime reports serving is recorded separately from the requested model name.\n62\t   V: 1) Find model call sites and their response handling. 2) Check the served version from the response or runtime (for example the response `model` field) is stored in its own field. 3) Answer no if only the requested name is recorded, or the served field is filled with the requested value.\n63\t## C06 G2 Re-test changed behavior | lead=agent_team\n64\tREQ: Before promotion, run typical allowed tasks and attack cases against the release candidate. Include destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping. Include retrieval, memory, and checker tests where they apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Keep the results for the exact candidate manifest.\n65\tNA: never\n66\tNOTCOV: Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed->C04; No tested rollback to an approved setup->C07; Config changes made without a reviewed diff and named author->C02\n67\t - C06.1 A CI release gate runs the test suite against the exact candidate (manifest digest and model IDs), with pass criteria set before the run, and blocks promotion on any blocking failure.\n68\t   V: 1) Open the CI workflow for release or promotion. 2) Check it runs the suite against the candidate's manifest digest and model IDs, with pass criteria in config before the run. 3) Check promotion depends on it. 4) Answer no if the job uses `continue-on-error`, `|| true`, or allowed failures, tests a different build, or promotion can run without it.\n69\t - C06.2 The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply.\n70\t   V: 1) List the test cases in the release suite. 2) Check each category exists: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist. 3) Answer no if any category is missing or its tests are skipped (`skip`, `xfail`, excluded markers).\n71\t - C06.3 Test results are stored linked to the exact candidate manifest.\n72\t   V: 1) Find where CI stores test results (artifacts, uploads, database). 2) Check each result is tagged with the candidate's manifest digest or type ID. 3) Answer no if results stay only in CI logs, are tagged only by branch or build number, or are not stored.\n73\t## C07 G2 Rollback and restart | lead=operations\n74\tREQ: Keep an approved recovery setup and show that rollback works. Confirm each hosted model it names is still offered, and record the announced retirement date. Account for changed credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work. Don't restore revoked permissions or replay unsafe side effects just because the old artifact still exists.\n75\tNA: never\n76\tNOTCOV: Changed or tampered checkpoints accepted, or unsafe replay of actions->R14; Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time->R11\n77\t - C07.1 An approved recovery setup is defined with references that can't change, and a rollback procedure or pipeline exists in code.\n78\t   V: 1) Find the recovery setup: a pinned earlier manifest or release record. 2) Check its references are digests or fixed versions. 3) Find a rollback script, pipeline, or runbook in the repo. 4) Answer no if references are tags or aliases, or no rollback procedure exists.\n79\t - C07.2 Credentials and permission grants are managed apart from the rolled-back artifacts, so a rollback does not restore revoked access.\n80\t   V: 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Check what it restores. 3) Answer no if it re-applies IAM policies, RoleBindings, secrets, or API keys from the old release, so revoked access comes back. Answer unknown if credentials are managed in another system and the repo does not show how.\n81\t - C07.3 The rollback procedure handles memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work explicitly (drain, migrate, or discard), so unsafe side effects are not replayed.\n82\t   V: 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Look for explicit steps for memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work: drain, migrate, or discard. 3) Answer no if any of these is not handled, or queued work is replayed as-is.\n83\t - C07.4 Each hosted model named in the recovery setup has a recorded retirement date.\n84\t   V: 1) List the hosted models named in the recovery setup. 2) Check each has a recorded retirement date. 3) Answer no if any hosted model lacks one.\n85\t## C08 G2 Connect configuration to execution | lead=operations\n86\tREQ: From a trace, look up the release manifest, the context sizes at each decision, and any prompts a parent passed to a sub-agent. Keep these links for the full audit retention period. Control access to sensitive artifacts.\n87\tNA: never\n88\tNOTCOV: Context size not measured or logged for each action decision->R07; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02; Manifest missing components that define the release->C01\n89\t - C08.1 Each trace or audit record carries the agent-type-id or manifest reference that resolves to the stored release manifest.\n90\t   V: 1) Open the trace or audit record writer. 2) Check every record carries `agent.type.id` or a manifest reference. 3) Check that value is the key of a stored manifest. 4) Answer no if the field is missing on some paths (errors, background jobs) or holds a value that resolves to no stored manifest.\n91\t - C08.2 The context size at each model decision is recorded in the trace.\n92\t   V: 1) Find model call sites and their spans. 2) Look for the context size recorded per call (for example `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` or a context-length attribute). 3) Answer no if it is missing on any model call path, including retries and sub-agents.\n93\t - C08.3 Prompts passed from a parent to a sub-agent are stored and linked from the trace.\n94\t   V: 1) Find sub-agent spawn code. 2) Check the parent's prompt is stored and the trace holds a link to it (ID, hash, or URI). 3) Answer no if the prompt is not stored, prompt capture is off, or the trace has no link.\n95\t - C08.4 Storage config keeps manifests, traces, and these links for at least the audit retention period, and limits access to sensitive artifacts by role.\n96\t   V: 1) Find storage config for manifests, traces, and prompt links: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention, TTLs. 2) Check retention covers the audit period for all of them. 3) Check IAM limits sensitive artifacts by role. 4) Answer no if any has a shorter TTL, no retention, or broad read access. Answer unknown if storage is configured outside this repo.\n97\t## E01 G2 Map shared responsibilities | lead=governance\n98\tREQ: List the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.\n99\tNA: never\n100\tNOTCOV: A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly->E06; A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records->E10; Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning->E02\n101\t - E01.1 An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n102\t   V: 1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.\n103\t - E01.2 Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner.\n104\t   V: 1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.\n105\t - E01.3 Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory.\n106\t   V: 1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.\n107\t## E02 G2 Vet and pin dependencies | lead=security\n108\tREQ: Before approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n109\tNA: never\n110\tNOTCOV: A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved->E03; Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata->E04; Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers->E05; Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked->B10\n111\t - E02.1 Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.\n112\t   V: 1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.\n113\t - E02.2 Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.\n114\t   V: 1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.\n115\t - E02.3 A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.\n116\t   V: 1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.\n117\t - E02.4 Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception.\n118\t   V: 1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.\n119\t## E03 G2 Re-check tools on load | lead=infrastructure\n120\tREQ: Take a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\n121\tNA: The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.\n122\tNOTCOV: Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved->E04; Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions->E02; A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one->E05; Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts->E06\n123\t - E03.1 A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\n124\t   V: 1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\n125\t - E03.2 The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\n126\t   V: 1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\n127\t - E03.3 When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\n128\t   V: 1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.\n129\t - E03.4 The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.\n130\t   V: 1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.\n131\t## E04 G2 Inspect tool metadata | lead=security\n132\tREQ: Before the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.\n133\tNA: The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.\n134\tNOTCOV: A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved->E03; Hidden instructions in tool call results->R01; Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers->E05; Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only->B01\n135\t - E04.1 A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.\n136\t   V: 1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.\n137\t - E04.2 The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.\n138\t   V: 1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.\n139\t - E04.3 Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.\n140\t   V: 1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.\n141\t - E04.4 A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.\n142\t   V: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.\n143\t## E05 G2 Verified, namespaced tools | lead=infrastructure\n144\tREQ: Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\n145\tNA: The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.\n146\tNOTCOV: Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata->E04; A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved->E03; Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message->E07; Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action->E06\n147\t - E05.1 Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.\n148\t   V: 1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.\n149\t - E05.2 The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n150\t   V: 1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.\n151\t - E05.3 Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n152\t   V: 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\n153\t## E06 G2 Enforced gateway boundary | lead=infrastructure\n154\tREQ: Send MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\n155\tNA: The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.\n156\tNOTCOV: Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool->B01; Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions->B02; Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval->B05; Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata->E04; Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations->B09\n157\t - E06.1 The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n158\t   V: 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n159\t - E06.2 The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n160\t   V: 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n161\t - E06.3 The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n162\t   V: 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n163\t - E06.4 If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n164\t   V: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n165\t - E06.5 Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n166\t   V: 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n167\t - E06.6 If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n168\t   V: 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n169\t## E07 G2 Untrusted peer messages | lead=agent_team\n170\tREQ: Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\n171\tNA: The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n172\tNOTCOV: Tool server identified only by its self-reported name->E05; Missing schema or size checks on API responses->R01; Peers or children still running after a stop->E09; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10\n173\t - E07.1 Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n174\t   V: 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n175\t - E07.2 Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n176\t   V: 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n177\t - E07.3 Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n178\t   V: 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n179\t - E07.4 Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n180\t   V: 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n181\t - E07.5 Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n182\t   V: 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n183\t## E08 G1 Bounded delegated authority | lead=agent_team\n184\tREQ: Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\n185\tNA: The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\n186\tNOTCOV: Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments->B06; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06; Children still running after the parent is stopped->E09; Replayed or faked peer-agent messages->E07; No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start->B07\n187\t - E08.1 Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n188\t   V: 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n189\t - E08.2 Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n190\t   V: 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n191\t - E08.3 An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n192\t   V: 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n193\t - E08.4 Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n194\t   V: 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n195\t## E09 G1 Recursive shutdown | lead=operations\n196\tREQ: Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\n197\tNA: never\n198\tNOTCOV: Agent able to disable or delay its own stop->R11; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12; Sub-agent given powers beyond its task->E08\n199\t - E09.1 A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n200\t   V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n201\t - E09.2 Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n202\t   V: 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n203\t - E09.3 Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n204\t   V: 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n205\t - E09.4 Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n206\t   V: 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n207\t## E10 G1 Audit across service boundaries | lead=infrastructure\n208\tREQ: Rebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n209\tNA: never\n210\tNOTCOV: Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11; Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record->R13; Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies->A04; Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident->R14\n211\t - E10.1 The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n212\t   V: 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n213\t - E10.2 The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n214\t   V: 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n215\t - E10.3 No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n216\t   V: 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n217\t - E10.4 Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n218\t   V: 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n219\t## E11 G2 Protect shared evidence | lead=infrastructure\n220\tREQ: Set rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\n221\tNA: never\n222\tNOTCOV: Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10; Run record missing denials, approvals, or results->R13; Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs->R03\n223\t - E11.1 Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n224\t   V: 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n225\t - E11.2 Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n226\t   V: 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n227\t - E11.3 Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n228\t   V: 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n229\t - E11.4 High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n230\t   V: 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n231\t## E12 G3 Fleet incident response | lead=security\n232\tREQ: Run a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n233\tNA: never\n234\tNOTCOV: Kill switch for a single agent not drilled->R11; No owner named on each side of a shared control->E01; Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning->E02; Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model->B07\n235\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9a932460-770f-4311-8553-70e3bee77013","uuid":"c8a8eb9b-f50c-4230-bb7d-c74023f0b3df","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:00:48.407Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-038tkh8l/agent/.ce.txt","content":"## C01 G2 Complete release manifest | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Write a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Running setup not checked against the approved manifest->C03; Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author->C02; Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed->C04; Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs->A03\n - C01.1 A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions.\n   V: 1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.\n - C01.2 The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version.\n   V: 1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.\n - C01.3 Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used.\n   V: 1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned.\n - C01.4 Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number.\n   V: 1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available.\n - C01.5 For training the team runs itself, the manifest records the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.\n   V: 1) Search for training or fine-tuning code (training jobs, `Trainer`, fine-tune API calls, pipeline configs). 2) If found, check the manifest records job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. 3) Answer no if training runs here and any field is missing. Answer unknown if training may run in another repo.\n## C02 G2 Review security-relevant changes | lead=governance\nREQ: Require diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies. Show who is allowed to change production config. Record emergency changes with an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed->C04; Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases->C06; No tested rollback to an approved setup->C07\n - C02.1 The manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files live in version control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author.\n   V: 1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service.\n - C02.2 Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration.\n   V: 1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment.\n - C02.3 An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n   V: 1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo.\n## C03 G1 Match release identity to running state | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Freeze the artifacts that define identity for each release. Check the running setup against the approved manifest, and emit the matching agent-type-id. Include the digest and signature of weights and adapters you host yourself. Test that a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup can't keep showing the old approved identity without being caught.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Manifest missing helper models, budgets, or other components->C01; Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs->A03; Container image not signature-checked at admission->B10\n - C03.1 Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags.\n   V: 1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one.\n - C03.2 A process outside the agent observes the running artifacts (image digest, prompt, model, tool setup) and compares them with the approved manifest, rather than trusting what the agent reports.\n   V: 1) Find a process outside the agent (admission controller, CI check, sidecar, scheduled job) that reads running artifacts. 2) Check it observes image digest, prompt, model, and tool setup itself and compares each with the approved manifest. 3) Answer no if it trusts values the agent reports, or compares a value with itself. Answer unknown if it would run in a platform outside this repo.\n - C03.3 The emitted agent-type-id is computed from the observed running artifacts, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup produces a different ID instead of the approved one.\n   V: 1) Find where the emitted `agent.type.id` comes from. 2) Check it is computed from the observed running artifacts at start or deploy. 3) Answer no if it is a constant, an env var or config value set at build, or copied from the approved manifest, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup would still emit the approved ID.\n - C03.4 Self-hosted weights and adapters are checked by digest and signature at load or deploy.\n   V: 1) Find where self-hosted weights and adapters are loaded or deployed (`from_pretrained`, `torch.load`, `safetensors`, download steps). 2) Check a digest compare and a signature check run before use. 3) Answer no if either is missing, wrapped in `try/except` that continues, or a second load path skips it. If only hosted models are used, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n - C03.5 A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it.\n   V: 1) Find the code that handles a manifest or digest mismatch. 2) Check it blocks or quarantines: non-zero exit, admission deny, refusal to start, scale to zero. 3) Answer no if the mismatch branch only logs, warns, or emits a metric and continues, or an error in the check lets deployment proceed.\n## C04 G2 Detect drift per agent | lead=operations\nREQ: Compare running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against their approved baseline. Simulate a prompt edit, an added tool, and a looser egress rule. Check that each one raises an alert and gets the documented response — block, quarantine, or rollback — within a set time.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; A tool server changing its description or schema after approval->E03; Who may change production config, and emergency change records->C02; Manifest missing components that define the release->C01\n - C04.1 A drift check compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against the approved baseline, with each of these covered.\n   V: 1) Find the drift check job or tool. 2) List what it compares: images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, self-hosted weights. 3) Check each is compared with the approved baseline, not with the live state itself. 4) Answer no if any category is missing or excluded. Answer unknown if drift detection runs in an outside platform.\n - C04.2 The drift check runs per agent deployment, on a schedule or on change events.\n   V: 1) Find what triggers the drift check: cron or CI schedule, admission webhook, or change events. 2) Check it runs for each agent deployment, not once for the fleet or only by hand. 3) Answer no if it is manual-only or covers only some deployments. Answer unknown if scheduling is set outside this repo.\n - C04.3 Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit.\n   V: 1) Open the drift check's result handling. 2) Check drift raises an alert and triggers a response set in config: block, quarantine, or rollback. 3) Check a time limit is set. 4) Answer no if drift only logs, the response or time limit is missing, or a check error is treated as no drift.\n## C05 G2 Control dependency, model, and training changes | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Pin dependency versions. Where supported, also pin each model's base version, checkpoint or weights, and fine-tune or adapter version. Before promotion, link any changed training run, dataset, or training code or config to the model it produced and to its test results. Record the model version actually served, when the runtime or provider shares it, separately from the name you requested. Before loading weights, adapters, or datasets you host, check their signature against an approved signer (for example, with OpenSSF Model Signing). Also check their file format. Pickle-based formats can run code when loaded, so they need an approved exception and a scan. Test swapping a model, checkpoint, or adapter, and check that it triggers review and re-testing. Some remote services or names can change behind a fixed name. For those, record missing training and version details clearly. Watch available version metadata and provider notices, and decide when to re-test or stop using the model. A local config hash alone can't prove remote behavior is unchanged. A pinned snapshot fixes the weights, not the provider's serving systems, which can change under the same ID. Keep scheduled re-testing.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Tool or MCP server approved without reviewing publisher, permissions, or vulnerabilities->E02; Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked->B10; Rollback setup naming a hosted model that is retired->C07\n - C05.1 Dependency versions are pinned with exact versions or hashes in lockfiles, with no ranges or floating tags.\n   V: 1) Open every dependency file and lockfile (`requirements*.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`) plus Dockerfile `FROM` and install lines. 2) Look for ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`, `*`), missing lockfiles, and floating tags. 3) Answer no if any dependency is unpinned, including install commands in Dockerfiles or CI scripts that bypass the lockfile.\n - C05.2 Model IDs in config are pinned snapshots (plus checkpoint, weights, and adapter versions where supported), not aliases such as 'latest'.\n   V: 1) Find every model ID in config, env files, and code defaults. 2) Follow env vars to their fallback value. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one when unset, or leaves out the checkpoint, weights, or adapter version where one is used and the provider supports pinning it.\n - C05.3 Self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets have their signatures checked against an approved signer before loading, and loading stops if the check fails.\n   V: 1) Find load paths for self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets. 2) Check a signature check against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing, `cosign verify` with a pinned identity) runs before loading. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, any signer is accepted, or a failure is caught and loading continues. If nothing is self-hosted, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n - C05.4 Pickle-based model formats are refused at load unless an approved exception and scan are recorded.\n   V: 1) Search for pickle-capable loads: `pickle.load`, `torch.load`, `joblib.load`, `.pkl`, `.pt`, `.bin` files. 2) Check the loader refuses these formats unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. 3) Answer no if any pickle-based load runs without that check.\n - C05.5 The model version the provider or runtime reports serving is recorded separately from the requested model name.\n   V: 1) Find model call sites and their response handling. 2) Check the served version from the response or runtime (for example the response `model` field) is stored in its own field. 3) Answer no if only the requested name is recorded, or the served field is filled with the requested value.\n## C06 G2 Re-test changed behavior | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Before promotion, run typical allowed tasks and attack cases against the release candidate. Include destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping. Include retrieval, memory, and checker tests where they apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Keep the results for the exact candidate manifest.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed->C04; No tested rollback to an approved setup->C07; Config changes made without a reviewed diff and named author->C02\n - C06.1 A CI release gate runs the test suite against the exact candidate (manifest digest and model IDs), with pass criteria set before the run, and blocks promotion on any blocking failure.\n   V: 1) Open the CI workflow for release or promotion. 2) Check it runs the suite against the candidate's manifest digest and model IDs, with pass criteria in config before the run. 3) Check promotion depends on it. 4) Answer no if the job uses `continue-on-error`, `|| true`, or allowed failures, tests a different build, or promotion can run without it.\n - C06.2 The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply.\n   V: 1) List the test cases in the release suite. 2) Check each category exists: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist. 3) Answer no if any category is missing or its tests are skipped (`skip`, `xfail`, excluded markers).\n - C06.3 Test results are stored linked to the exact candidate manifest.\n   V: 1) Find where CI stores test results (artifacts, uploads, database). 2) Check each result is tagged with the candidate's manifest digest or type ID. 3) Answer no if results stay only in CI logs, are tagged only by branch or build number, or are not stored.\n## C07 G2 Rollback and restart | lead=operations\nREQ: Keep an approved recovery setup and show that rollback works. Confirm each hosted model it names is still offered, and record the announced retirement date. Account for changed credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work. Don't restore revoked permissions or replay unsafe side effects just because the old artifact still exists.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Changed or tampered checkpoints accepted, or unsafe replay of actions->R14; Hosted model changing behind a fixed name->C05; Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time->R11\n - C07.1 An approved recovery setup is defined with references that can't change, and a rollback procedure or pipeline exists in code.\n   V: 1) Find the recovery setup: a pinned earlier manifest or release record. 2) Check its references are digests or fixed versions. 3) Find a rollback script, pipeline, or runbook in the repo. 4) Answer no if references are tags or aliases, or no rollback procedure exists.\n - C07.2 Credentials and permission grants are managed apart from the rolled-back artifacts, so a rollback does not restore revoked access.\n   V: 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Check what it restores. 3) Answer no if it re-applies IAM policies, RoleBindings, secrets, or API keys from the old release, so revoked access comes back. Answer unknown if credentials are managed in another system and the repo does not show how.\n - C07.3 The rollback procedure handles memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work explicitly (drain, migrate, or discard), so unsafe side effects are not replayed.\n   V: 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Look for explicit steps for memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work: drain, migrate, or discard. 3) Answer no if any of these is not handled, or queued work is replayed as-is.\n - C07.4 Each hosted model named in the recovery setup has a recorded retirement date.\n   V: 1) List the hosted models named in the recovery setup. 2) Check each has a recorded retirement date. 3) Answer no if any hosted model lacks one.\n## C08 G2 Connect configuration to execution | lead=operations\nREQ: From a trace, look up the release manifest, the context sizes at each decision, and any prompts a parent passed to a sub-agent. Keep these links for the full audit retention period. Control access to sensitive artifacts.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Context size not measured or logged for each action decision->R07; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02; Manifest missing components that define the release->C01\n - C08.1 Each trace or audit record carries the agent-type-id or manifest reference that resolves to the stored release manifest.\n   V: 1) Open the trace or audit record writer. 2) Check every record carries `agent.type.id` or a manifest reference. 3) Check that value is the key of a stored manifest. 4) Answer no if the field is missing on some paths (errors, background jobs) or holds a value that resolves to no stored manifest.\n - C08.2 The context size at each model decision is recorded in the trace.\n   V: 1) Find model call sites and their spans. 2) Look for the context size recorded per call (for example `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` or a context-length attribute). 3) Answer no if it is missing on any model call path, including retries and sub-agents.\n - C08.3 Prompts passed from a parent to a sub-agent are stored and linked from the trace.\n   V: 1) Find sub-agent spawn code. 2) Check the parent's prompt is stored and the trace holds a link to it (ID, hash, or URI). 3) Answer no if the prompt is not stored, prompt capture is off, or the trace has no link.\n - C08.4 Storage config keeps manifests, traces, and these links for at least the audit retention period, and limits access to sensitive artifacts by role.\n   V: 1) Find storage config for manifests, traces, and prompt links: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention, TTLs. 2) Check retention covers the audit period for all of them. 3) Check IAM limits sensitive artifacts by role. 4) Answer no if any has a shorter TTL, no retention, or broad read access. Answer unknown if storage is configured outside this repo.\n## E01 G2 Map shared responsibilities | lead=governance\nREQ: List the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly->E06; A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records->E10; Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning->E02\n - E01.1 An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n   V: 1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.\n - E01.2 Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner.\n   V: 1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.\n - E01.3 Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory.\n   V: 1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.\n## E02 G2 Vet and pin dependencies | lead=security\nREQ: Before approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved->E03; Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata->E04; Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers->E05; Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked->B10\n - E02.1 Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.\n   V: 1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.\n - E02.2 Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.\n   V: 1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.\n - E02.3 A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.\n   V: 1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.\n - E02.4 Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception.\n   V: 1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.\n## E03 G2 Re-check tools on load | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Take a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\nNA: The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.\nNOTCOV: Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved->E04; Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions->E02; A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one->E05; Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts->E06\n - E03.1 A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\n   V: 1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\n - E03.2 The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\n   V: 1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\n - E03.3 When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\n   V: 1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.\n - E03.4 The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.\n   V: 1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.\n## E04 G2 Inspect tool metadata | lead=security\nREQ: Before the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.\nNA: The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.\nNOTCOV: A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved->E03; Hidden instructions in tool call results->R01; Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers->E05; Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only->B01\n - E04.1 A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.\n   V: 1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.\n - E04.2 The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.\n   V: 1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.\n - E04.3 Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.\n   V: 1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.\n - E04.4 A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.\n   V: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.\n## E05 G2 Verified, namespaced tools | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\nNA: The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.\nNOTCOV: Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata->E04; A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved->E03; Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message->E07; Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action->E06\n - E05.1 Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.\n   V: 1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.\n - E05.2 The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n   V: 1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.\n - E05.3 Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n   V: 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\n## E06 G2 Enforced gateway boundary | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Send MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\nNA: The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.\nNOTCOV: Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool->B01; Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions->B02; Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval->B05; Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata->E04; Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations->B09\n - E06.1 The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n   V: 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n - E06.2 The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n   V: 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n - E06.3 The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n   V: 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n - E06.4 If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n   V: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n - E06.5 Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n   V: 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n - E06.6 If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n   V: 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n## E07 G2 Untrusted peer messages | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\nNA: The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\nNOTCOV: Tool server identified only by its self-reported name->E05; Missing schema or size checks on API responses->R01; Peers or children still running after a stop->E09; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10\n - E07.1 Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n   V: 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n - E07.2 Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n   V: 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n - E07.3 Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n   V: 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n - E07.4 Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n   V: 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n - E07.5 Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n   V: 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n## E08 G1 Bounded delegated authority | lead=agent_team\nREQ: Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\nNA: The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\nNOTCOV: Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments->B06; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06; Children still running after the parent is stopped->E09; Replayed or faked peer-agent messages->E07; No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start->B07\n - E08.1 Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n   V: 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n - E08.2 Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n   V: 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n - E08.3 An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n   V: 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n - E08.4 Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n   V: 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n## E09 G1 Recursive shutdown | lead=operations\nREQ: Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Agent able to disable or delay its own stop->R11; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12; Sub-agent given powers beyond its task->E08\n - E09.1 A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n   V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n - E09.2 Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n   V: 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n - E09.3 Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n   V: 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n - E09.4 Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n   V: 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n## E10 G1 Audit across service boundaries | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Rebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11; Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record->R13; Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies->A04; Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident->R14\n - E10.1 The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n   V: 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n - E10.2 The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n   V: 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n - E10.3 No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n   V: 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n - E10.4 Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n   V: 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n## E11 G2 Protect shared evidence | lead=infrastructure\nREQ: Set rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10; Run record missing denials, approvals, or results->R13; Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs->R03\n - E11.1 Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n   V: 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n - E11.2 Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n   V: 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n - E11.3 Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n   V: 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n - E11.4 High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n   V: 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n## E12 G3 Fleet incident response | lead=security\nREQ: Run a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\nNA: never\nNOTCOV: Kill switch for a single agent not drilled->R11; No owner named on each side of a shared control->E01; Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning->E02; Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model->B07\n","numLines":235,"startLine":1,"totalLines":235}}}
